\relax 
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Massive-STEPS cities. \emph  {Queries}: test next-visit queries kept by the STHGCN protocol (target POI and user seen in training). \emph  {Revisit}: share of check-ins to an already visited place, over all check-ins and over the scored queries. $\mitgamma $: novelty exponent of $P(\text  {new})\propto S^{-\mitgamma }$.\relax }}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:datasets}{{1}{1}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What transfers across cities.} (a)\nobreakspace  {}Place identifiers are disjoint between cities (cross-city Jensen--Shannon divergence $=1$ bit); the more abstract the description of a visit, the more cities share it. (b)\nobreakspace  {}The novelty exponent $\mitgamma $ of the exploration law $P(\text  {new})\propto S^{-\mitgamma }$ in the 14 evaluated cities; the orange band is the gate coefficient $-g_S$ that \textsc  {Gemma}{} learns on the six source cities without ever being given $\mitgamma $. (c)\nobreakspace  {}Accuracy on the eight cities \textsc  {Gemma}{} never saw: zero-shot \textsc  {Gemma}{} is within 0.3 points of STHGCN, which is trained in each city, and nearly doubles the best LLM agent; light fine-tuning on the training split exceeds STHGCN.\relax }}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:teaser}{{1}{2}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Next-POI prediction on the eight cities GEMMA never saw during training (STHGCN protocol; baselines from Massive-STEPS). \emph  {Target train}: the model is trained on that city's training split. GEMMA zero-shot uses no data from the city except each test user's own past check-ins; fine-tuned GEMMA uses the training split only. Mean of 3 seeds. Best in \textbf  {bold}, second \underline  {underlined}. A@1 = Acc@1, N@5 = NDCG@5; pooled = weighted by the number of test queries.\relax }}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main-unseen}{{2}{2}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Next-POI prediction on the six source cities GEMMA was trained on (in-domain; fine-tuned GEMMA, 3 seeds).\relax }}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main-source}{{3}{2}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {\textsc  {Gemma}.} (a)\nobreakspace  {}Check-ins are tokens built from time, relative geometry, the activity taxonomy and a language embedding of the place, never from a place identifier. (b)\nobreakspace  {}A Transformer encodes the user's history into states $\mathbf {h}_{1:t}$ and a next-step latent $\mathbf {z}$. (c)\nobreakspace  {}Parameter-free statistics of the user's own history score every candidate place. (d)\nobreakspace  {}The next-place distribution factorises exactly into three decisions: the activity, a gate between returning and exploring that follows the exploration--preferential-return law, and the place, scored by non-negative, context-modulated evidence weights. (e)\nobreakspace  {}All parameters act on statistics, so the model trained on six cities is applied unchanged to unseen cities.\relax }}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:arch}{{2}{3}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Zero-shot comparison with LLM agents under the LLM-Move protocol of Massive-STEPS: candidates are the true next place plus 100 random places of the city, history = the user's last 15 trajectories, Acc@1. LLM rows are the published numbers (200 users); GEMMA is the mean over 3 seeds $\times $ 5 user samples of 200 users. GEMMA uses no LLM at inference.\relax }}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:llm}{{4}{3}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Per-city difference to STHGCN} (Acc@1 points, 95\% CI from test sampling and seed spread; number of test queries in brackets). (a)\nobreakspace  {}On unseen cities, zero-shot \textsc  {Gemma}{} (hollow) is statistically indistinguishable from STHGCN in 6 of 8 cities (better in Tangerang, worse in Sydney); fine-tuned \textsc  {Gemma}{} (solid) is indistinguishable in 7 and significantly better in Tangerang, with no significant loss. (b)\nobreakspace  {}On the six source cities STHGCN keeps an in-domain advantage in Istanbul, Petaling Jaya and Kuwait City; \textsc  {Gemma}{} is better in Jakarta and New York.\relax }}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:forest}{{3}{4}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Zero-shot \textsc  {Gemma}{} vs.\ LLM agents} under the LLM-Move protocol. Blue: the best of the 15 published LLM agents in each city (an oracle choice of framework and model); orange: \textsc  {Gemma}{} (3 seeds), which runs no LLM.\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:llm}{{4}{5}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Aggregate results on the eight unseen cities. Fine-tuned GEMMA is shown with the test-time history drawn from the training split only (as STHGCN) and from train+validation.\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:aggregate}{{5}{5}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Ablations: change of zero-shot macro Acc@1 (points, eight unseen cities).\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{6}{5}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Learned parameters (at $\mathbf {z}=0$). Source: trained on six cities, mean $\pm $ sd over 3 seeds. Fine-tuned: range over the eight unseen cities $\times $ 3 seeds after fine-tuning. $^\dagger $\,needs the target-city log, inactive in zero-shot.\relax }}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:weights}{{7}{5}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Anatomy of the prediction.} (a)\nobreakspace  {}Zero-shot Acc@1 split into returns ($\mitrho A_R$) and explorations ($(1-\mitrho )A_E$); almost all hits are returns. (b)\nobreakspace  {}Ranking a \emph  {new} place, given that the user explores (mean over the 8 unseen cities): \textsc  {Gemma}{} beats distance and popularity rankers, but oracles that know the true activity or category (*, hatched) show how much remains. (c)\nobreakspace  {}Single signals ranked alone: the user's own frequency, time-of-day and transitions carry most of the information; hatched signals need a log of the target city.\relax }}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:anatomy}{{5}{6}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Learned parameters.} Bars: values learned on the six source cities (mean $\pm $ sd over 3 seeds). Markers: values after fine-tuning in each unseen (circles) or source (squares) city. Fine-tuning changes the return weights by a median of 8\% (relative $L_1$); only the largest cities move further, mostly through the is-last penalty. (c)\nobreakspace  {}The gate's coefficient on $\qopname  \relax o{log}S_u$ falls inside the range of $-\mitgamma $ measured in the 14 evaluated cities (grey band: inter-quartile range).\relax }}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:weights}{{6}{6}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Ablations (zero-shot).} (a)\nobreakspace  {}Learning the evidence weights on source cities instead of setting them by hand adds 2.5 points (macro over 8 unseen cities). (b)\nobreakspace  {}Removing representation-learning objectives from the backbone changes accuracy by less than 0.1 point on average: the transfer does not come from pretraining the encoder.\relax }}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ablation}{{7}{7}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Fine-tuning dynamics.} Validation Acc@1 relative to the transferred model (epoch 0). Gains are small and arrive within the first epochs.\relax }}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:finetune}{{8}{7}}
